{"title":"Introduction to Programming Week 11","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Summary\">Summary</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Introduction_to_Programming_Week_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Summary\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Just like last week, we've been working primarily on our large <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Pass_the_Pigs&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Pass the Pigs (page does not exist)\">Pass the Pigs</a> project. See the transcript for details.\n</p><p>Otherwise, the only thing new this week is something called <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=String_formatting&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"String formatting (page does not exist)\">string formatting</a>.  The usual way to combine <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=String\" title=\"String\">strings</a> is with concatenation, but Python also provides string formatting, which is sometimes more flexible.  Neither is strictly better than the other, use whichever you prefer.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Introduction_to_Programming_Week_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Introduction_to_Programming_Week_10\" title=\"Introduction to Programming Week 10\">Introduction to Programming Week 10</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Introduction_to_Programming_Week_12&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Introduction to Programming Week 12 (page does not exist)\">Introduction to Programming Week 12</a></li></ul>\n<!-- \nNew